							<h1 class="stat-page-number">68% vs. 24% Relief</h1>
							<h2 class="stat-page-title">Treating the Trigger: Peripheral Barrier Sealing vs. Central Nerve&nbsp;Pills</h2>
							<p class="stat-page-desc">
								In systematic reviews of pharmacological clinical trials for BMS (Patton et al., 2007; McMillan et al., 2016), central neuromodulating medications (clonazepam, gabapentin, tricyclic antidepressants) achieve meaningful pain reduction in only 20% to 28% of patients (mean 24.1%), often with systemic side effects like somnolence. In contrast, clinical cohorts that target the peripheral root trigger (desensitizing exposed dentin, resealing crown margins, and eliminating oral acid) achieve meaningful pain reduction in 68% of adherent patients (52 of 76&nbsp;individuals).
							</p>
							<div class="stat-page-source">
								<a href="#src-30" class="table-src-link">Response Comparison: 68% peripheral vs. 24.1% central &bull; Sources: Patton et al. (2007); McMillan et al. (2016)</a>
