<!-- Chapter 4: BMS History and Evolution (Page 1 of 3: From Glossodynia to Neuropathy) -->
			<section class="book-page" id="page-3c">
				<div id="chapter-4" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
				<div id="chapter-3b" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
				<div id="chapter-17" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
				<div id="ch17-algorithm" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
				<div id="ch17-consensus" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
				<div id="ch17-histology" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
				<div id="ch17-stigma" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>

				<div class="page-body-content">
										<div class="chapter-opener-header">
						<div class="chapter-opener-num">Chapter 4</div>
						<h2 class="chapter-title chapter-opener-title">BMS History and&nbsp;Evolution</h2>
						<div class="chapter-opener-divider"></div>
					</div>

					<div class="chapter-content is-first-page">
						<p>To appreciate how modern clinicians navigate the intricate diagnosis of Burning Mouth Syndrome, it is helpful to explore the historical journey of oral sensory science. For generations, because oral tissues consistently appeared completely healthy under visual examination, medicine lacked the microscopic tools to visualize small-fiber nerve endings or sub-micron chemical stimuli. As neuroscience and diagnostic technology evolved, dedicated researchers and clinicians progressively unlocked the neurobiological foundations of oral&nbsp;burning.</p>

						<h3>1900 to 1950: Early Clinical Documentation of&nbsp;"Glossodynia"</h3>
						<p>From 1900 to 1950, chronic oral burning was clinically documented under terms like <em>glossodynia</em> (painful tongue) or <em>stomatodynia</em> (painful mouth). In an era preceding electron microscopy and receptor pharmacology, physical inspection was limited to macroscopic tissue evaluations. Because the oral mucosa consistently presented as intact, healthy, and pink, early medical frameworks often categorized these sensations as functional or stress-related responses, reflecting the diagnostic boundaries of the era. This clinical curiosity ultimately inspired subsequent generations of oral medicine specialists to search for deeper neuro-sensory&nbsp;mechanisms.</p>

						<h3>1988 to 1996: The Lamey Clinical&nbsp;Triad</h3>
						<p>The landmark clinical classification of Burning Mouth Syndrome was established between 1988 and 1996 by Scottish oral medicine specialist Dr. Philip-John Lamey (University of Glasgow and School of Dentistry, Belfast) through foundational studies published in the <em>British Medical Journal</em> (Lamey &amp; Lamb 1988 <a href="#src-31" class="cite-ref">[31]</a>), the <em>British Dental Journal</em> (Lamey &amp; Lewis 1989 <a href="#src-31" class="cite-ref">[31]</a>), and the <em>Journal of Oral Pathology &amp; Medicine</em> (Lamey et al. 1994 <a href="#src-32" class="cite-ref">[32]</a>). Lamey introduced the first rigorous clinical taxonomy, dividing BMS into three diurnal subtypes based on how pain behaves from morning to&nbsp;night:</p>
						<ul class="chapter-list">
							<li><strong>Type 1 (33% to 35% of patients):</strong> Symptom-free upon waking. Burning sensations ignite by mid-morning and steadily climb to peak intensity by evening (Lamey &amp; Lewis <a href="#src-31" class="cite-ref">[31]</a>). Closely linked to dietary triggers, salivary acid clearance, and exposed tooth root margins.</li>
							<li><strong>Type 2 (55% of patients):</strong> Continuous, unrelenting burning from the instant of awakening to sleep. Highly refractory, tracking severe baseline nerve hyperexcitability and sleep disturbances (Lamey <a href="#src-33" class="cite-ref">[33]</a>).</li>
							<li><strong>Type 3 (10% to 12% of patients):</strong> Intermittent, erratic burning with pain-free days, frequently localized to atypical mucosal areas and triggered by specific food contacts, additives, or episodic gastric reflux (Lamey et al. <a href="#src-32" class="cite-ref">[32]</a>).</li>
						</ul>

						<h3>1990s to 2004: Primary vs. Secondary BMS and&nbsp;ICHD-II</h3>
						<p>By 2004, the International Headache Society formally codified BMS in the <em>International Classification of Headache Disorders (ICHD-II)</em> as code 13.18.5 <a href="#src-35" class="cite-ref">[35]</a>. International consensus divided oral burning into two clinical&nbsp;categories:</p>
						<ul class="chapter-list">
							<li><strong>Secondary BMS:</strong> Oral burning resulting directly from an identifiable macroscopic medical cause, such as candidiasis fungal infection, Sj&ouml;gren's syndrome, poorly controlled diabetes, severe B12/folate anemia, or medication side effects like ACE inhibitors (Scala et al. <a href="#src-34" class="cite-ref">[34]</a>; Patton et al. <a href="#src-34" class="cite-ref">[34]</a>). When the underlying systemic driver is treated, the burning&nbsp;resolves.</li>
							<li><strong>Primary BMS (True BMS):</strong> Idiopathic oral burning where all routine clinical examinations, laboratory blood panels, and oral tissue biopsies return completely normal (ICHD-II 2004; ICOP 2020 &sect;6.1.1 <a href="#src-35" class="cite-ref">[35]</a>). Under classical frameworks, Primary BMS was viewed as an insoluble mystery. Modern methodology, however, recognizes that "Primary BMS" corresponds directly to Lamey's <strong>Type 1 diurnal pattern</strong>: patients wake up completely symptom-free because resting nocturnal saliva buffers acids, but experience progressive burning through the day as everyday dietary acids and biofilm bacterial acids touch thinned barriers and exposed tooth necks. Modern protocol focuses entirely on this "true BMS" cohort by eliminating acid triggers and sealing exposed hardware.</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="page-number-footer">
					<span>14</span>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- Chapter 4: BMS History and Evolution (Page 2 of 3: Small-Fiber Neuropathy Discovery) -->
			<section class="book-page" id="page-3d">
				<div class="page-body-content">
					<div class="page-running-head">
						<span class="chapter-header-label">Chapter 4: BMS History and Evolution</span>
						<span class="page-count-label">Page 14b</span>
					</div>

					<div class="chapter-content">
						<h3>2005 to 2017 Breakthrough: Histological Proof of Small-Fiber&nbsp;Neuropathy</h3>
						<p>In 2005, Dr. Giuseppe Lauria and colleagues published a landmark study in <em>PAIN</em> that definitively established the neuro-anatomical foundation of the&nbsp;condition. By taking microscopic epithelial punch biopsies of the anterior tongue in BMS patients and staining for PGP 9.5 (protein gene product 9.5, an axonal marker), Lauria proved that BMS patients had a <strong>statistically massive loss of intraepithelial nerve fibers</strong> compared to healthy matched controls.</p>
						<p>The surviving nerve fibers were not dead: they were axonal sprouts, raw, hyperexcitable, denuded unmyelinated C-fibers and thin A-delta fibers firing spontaneous ectopic pain signals. Subsequent functional MRI (fMRI) studies by Albuquerque (2006) and J&auml;&auml;skel&auml;inen (2012) confirmed altered cerebral pain processing and loss of central dopaminergic inhibition. BMS was definitively proven to be a <strong>tangible small-fiber neuropathy of the trigeminal system</strong>.</p>

						<h3>2020 ICOP Consensus: Codifying Orofacial&nbsp;Pain</h3>
						<p>In 2020, the first comprehensive <em>International Classification of Orofacial Pain (ICOP 1st Edition)</em> codified Primary BMS under code 6.1.1: <em>"An intraoral burning or dysaesthetic sensation, recurring daily for more than 2 hours per day over more than 3 months, without clinically evident causative lesions."</em> This unified dentistry, neurology, and ENT worldwide under a single diagnostic standard.</p>

						<h3>2026 Diagnostic Shift: The ICOP-AL Validation &amp; Legacy&nbsp;Flaws</h3>
						<p>In early 2026, researchers published the multi-center <strong>ICOP-AL validation study</strong>, evaluating the diagnostic accuracy of the 2020 criteria in tertiary clinical centers. While the algorithm achieved an outstanding 95.8% diagnostic specificity, the study highlighted two valuable opportunities to expand clinical evaluation beyond conventional&nbsp;boundaries:</p>
						<ol class="chapter-list">
							<li><strong>The Diagnostic Plateau of Exclusion:</strong> Under existing ICOP and ICHD criteria, Burning Mouth Syndrome is categorized as a diagnosis of exclusion. Once visual inspection, biopsies, and blood panels rule out systemic pathology, clinicians reach the boundary of standard medical workups. Without specialized interdisciplinary tools to test for sub-micron cement hydrolysis or localized salivary pH dynamics, the diagnostic process naturally halts at "primary idiopathic," leaving peripheral micro-triggers uninvestigated.</li>
							<li><strong>The Unmeasured Micro-Environment:</strong> Conventional guidelines test systemic blood markers but fail to measure localized salivary pH dynamics, bacterial biofilm fermentation, or microscopic dentin tubule exposure adjacent to the burning tongue.</li>
						</ol>

						<h3>2026 Consensus: Renaming BMS to Burning Mouth Disorder&nbsp;(BMD)</h3>
						<p>Following these diagnostic validations, an international Delphi consensus study published in <em>PAIN</em> reached an agreement to formally rename Burning Mouth Syndrome to <strong>Burning Mouth Disorder (BMD)</strong> for the upcoming ICD-11 criteria. The expert panel concluded that the label "syndrome" erroneously implies a vague collection of disparate symptoms, whereas oral burning is a defined neuro-sensory disturbance. Crucially, the consensus voted to eliminate the requirement for emotional distress and arbitrary daily hours, establishing that oral burning must be evaluated as an objective biological pain condition rather than a psychiatric&nbsp;diagnosis.</p>
					</div>
				</div>

