<!-- Chapter 5: My Story and My Discoveries (Page 1 of 9: Episode 1) -->
			<section class="book-page" id="page-15">
				<div id="chapter-5" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
				<div id="chapter-4-story" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
				<div id="ch4-aba" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
				<div id="ch4-episodes" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
				<div id="ch4-recent" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>

				<div class="page-body-content">
										<div class="chapter-opener-header">
						<div class="chapter-opener-num">Chapter 5</div>
						<h2 class="chapter-title chapter-opener-title">My Story and My&nbsp;Discoveries</h2>
						<div class="chapter-opener-divider"></div>
					</div>

					<div class="chapter-content is-first-page">
						<p>When doctors and dentists label Burning Mouth Syndrome as an incurable idiopathic mystery, your only rational path as a patient is to stop guessing and treat your biology as an engineering circuit. My journey out of 10/10 chronic pain was not a straight downward line. It was an escalating series of distinct episodes over four years, where eliminating a specific input produced total relief, only for the fire to return months later through a different&nbsp;pathway.</p>

						<!-- Universal Interactive BMS Pain Progression Graph (From Homepage & Post #1) -->
						<div class="book-progression-graph-box" style="margin: 0.85rem 0 1.25rem; width: 100%;">
							<InteractiveProgressionChart />
						</div>

						<div id="ep1-beer" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
						<h3>Episode #1: Eliminating Beer &amp; Wine (Approx. 4 Years&nbsp;Ago)</h3>
						<p>My symptoms first erupted with intense oral scalding and bilateral tongue aching following dark beer, ales, and wine. At the time, I had never heard of BMS and assumed it was an isolated beverage sensitivity. I eliminated all beer and wine completely, and within weeks, the burning dropped back to a zero out of ten. For roughly six months, I was 100% pain-free and believed the nightmare was&nbsp;over.</p>
						<p><strong>The ABAB Reversal Test:</strong> I am completely certain beer was the causal trigger because I did not rely on coincidence: I conducted a clinical ABAB reversal test. Baseline (A): regular craft beer produced severe burning. Intervention (B): stopping beer returned pain to zero for six months. Reversal (A): re-introducing beer caused the exact same fire to return within 48 to 72 hours. Re-intervention (B): cutting beer again extinguished the pain completely. The biological link was&nbsp;conclusive.</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="page-number-footer">
					<span>15</span>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- Chapter 5: My Story and My Discoveries (Page 2 of 9: Episode 2) -->
			<section class="book-page" id="page-16">
				<div class="page-body-content">
					<div class="page-running-head">
						<span class="chapter-header-label">Part II &bull; Chapter 5: My Story and My Discoveries</span>
						<span class="page-count-label">Page 16</span>
					</div>

					<div class="chapter-content">
						<div id="ep2-coffee" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
						<h3>Episode #2: Eliminating Coffee (The Second&nbsp;Flare)</h3>
						<p>Six months later, out of nowhere, the fire returned at an eight out of ten. By methodically logging my daily routine, I isolated the new culprit: daily coffee. The organic acids and roasting compounds were irritating sensitized mucosal tissues. Just like with beer, I ran an ABAB reversal test: eliminating coffee returned my mouth to a stable zero out of ten baseline for another six months pain-free. Re-introducing it brought the burn back; cutting it silenced the nerve alarm&nbsp;again.</p>

						<div id="ch6-spotless-labs" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
						<h4 class="episode-subheading">The "Spotless Lab Paradox" &amp; The Diagnostic&nbsp;Conundrum</h4>
						<p>When the pain flared uncontrollably between dietary changes, I consulted primary care physicians, ENTs, and neurologists. Every standard diagnostic test came back completely normal: complete blood counts, ferritin, vitamin B12, autoimmune panels (ANA, Anti-SSA/SSB), inflammatory markers (CRP, ESR), and brain MRI and head CT scans. To modern medicine, I was the picture of perfect health, yet my mouth felt like it was immersed in boiling&nbsp;oil.</p>
						<p>Because physical inspection reveals intact pink mucosa and routine systemic labs return normal, clinical guidelines direct specialists toward central neuromodulators: clonazepam, gabapentin, or amitriptyline. These medications are designed to dial down central gain and dampen dorsal horn wind-up. While they provide meaningful coping relief for patients with intense central amplification, they are not engineered to detect or seal peripheral micro-chemical leaks at the tooth&nbsp;margin.</p>

						<div class="book-image-container" style="margin: 0.75rem 0 0.45rem; text-align: center;">
