						<h3>Episode #6: The Two-Week Carnivore&nbsp;Trial</h3>
						<p>In mid-2024, after analyzing clinical literature on fermentable carbohydrates and oral bacterial acidogenesis, I initiated a strict two-week carnivore elimination trial: eating exclusively pasture-raised ruminant proteins (beef and lamb), animal fats, sea salt, and pure water. The results were immediate and startling: within 96 hours, my burning pain dropped from an agonizing 8/10 baseline down to a mild&nbsp;1/10.</p>

						<h4 class="episode-subheading">The Biological Mechanism: Starving Bacterial Lactic&nbsp;Acid</h4>
						<p>Unlike plants, fruits, or grains, whole animal proteins and fats deliver <strong>zero fermentable carbohydrates</strong> into the oral cavity. Oral bacteria such as <em>Streptococcus mutans</em> and <em>Lactobacillus</em> require dietary sugars and starches to fuel glycolysis. Deprived of fermentable substrates, these bacteria cannot synthesize lactic acid. Resting salivary pH, which routinely plunged below 5.8 after carbohydrate consumption, stabilized at an optimal neutral-to-alkaline baseline of 7.2 to&nbsp;7.4.</p>

						<h4 class="episode-subheading">Diagnostic Baseline vs. Nutritional&nbsp;Ideology</h4>
						<p>I did not approach this trial as an internet wellness trend or a permanent ideological crusade. In Single-Case Experimental Design (SCED), this served as an essential <strong>diagnostic elimination baseline</strong>. When your mouth feels like it is on fire, you cannot determine whether you are reacting to dietary citric acid, dental composite leaching, or food allergens if bacterial acid is continuously bathing your exposed tubules. By dropping oral lactic acid production to zero, my raw, denuded mucosal nerve terminals finally received the resting environment required to cease spontaneous ectopic firing and initiate tissue&nbsp;repair.</p>

						<h4 class="episode-subheading">The First Sensation of Physical&nbsp;Remission</h4>
						<p>For the first time in four agonizing years, I woke up in the morning without the sensation of having scalded my tongue on boiling soup. Speaking without friction, chewing without dread, and sleeping through the night without throbbing nerve pain proved that my chronic burning was not primary psychogenic somatization: it was a direct electrochemical reaction to localized oral&nbsp;chemistry.</p>
