					<div class="chapter-content">
						<h3>The Two Neural Cables: Sharp Zing vs. Slow Burn</h3>
						<p>To understand which receptors fire and why, we must first look at the two distinct types of sensory nerve fibers that wire the teeth, gums, and oral&nbsp;mucosa:</p>

						<p><strong>The Normal Zing (A&delta; Fibers):</strong> These are thinly myelinated, rapid-conducting sensory fibers (conducting at 5 to 30 meters per second) located right at the pulp-dentin border and projecting into the inner third of dentinal tubules. They respond primarily to hydrodynamic fluid shearing: the physical push and pull of fluid rushing across odontoblast processes. When you bite into ice cream or breathe cold winter air, fluid jerks inside open tubules, mechanically stretching the A&delta; nerve endings. You feel a sharp, instantaneous 2-second electric jolt, and the moment the stimulus stops, the signal goes quiet. That is normal, healthy dental mechanoreception.</p>

						<p><strong>The Slow Burn (Unmyelinated C-Fibers):</strong> These fibers are located deeper in the dental pulp core and weave densely through the subodontoblastic plexus, periodontal ligament, and oral mucosa. They are polymodal, meaning their membrane surfaces are studded with chemical, thermal, and mechanical transducers that respond to noxious heat, bacterial toxins, inflammatory mediators, and sustained tissue acidosis. Unlike A&delta; fibers, C-fibers lack a myelin sheath and conduct at a crawl (0.5 to 2.0 meters per second).</p>

						<p>When exposed root collars or compromised restoration borders are left unsealed and continually bathed in oral acids and bacterial lactic acid, chronic subclinical inflammation sets in. As tissue pH drops below neutral, the activation threshold for these slow-conducting C-fibers drops dramatically, and they become chronically recruited. In human neurophysiology, C-fiber firing does not produce a sharp electric shock: by biological definition, sustained C-fiber depolarization produces a slow, dull, aching, or caustic <strong>burning sensation</strong>.</p>

						<h3>Speed, Reliability &amp; Electrical Cross-Talk</h3>
						<p>Because unmyelinated C-fibers travel bundled tightly inside common Schwann cell sheaths (known as Remak bundles) without individual myelin insulation, sustained firing creates potassium ion (K<sup>+</sup>) accumulation in the microscopic extracellular gaps. Action potentials in one active C-fiber create local voltage fields that directly depolarize silent neighboring fibers without any anatomical synapse. Through this phenomenon of <strong>ephaptic transmission (cross-talk)</strong>, a sustained acid fire originating at a single unsealed tooth margin reliably radiates outward, lighting up adjacent tongue and mucosal pathways.</p>
					</div>
				</div>

