						<span class="chapter-header-label">Part III &bull; Chapter 10: How Pain Is Created</span>
						<span class="page-count-label">Page 28a2</span>
					</div>

					<div class="chapter-content">
						<h3>What Science Had Already Mapped</h3>
						<p>In academic neurophysiology and endodontic research laboratories, scientists have documented several foundational realities for decades:</p>
						<ul>
							<li><strong>The Fibers Produce Different Sensations:</strong> Classic sensory neurobiology (dating back to researchers like Jyv&auml;sj&auml;rvi, N&auml;rhi, and Byers in the 1980s and 1990s) proved that while myelinated A&delta; fibers create sharp, localized pricking pain, unmyelinated pulpal C-fibers produce dull, radiating, burning pain.</li>
							<li><strong>Acid Targets Thermal Receptors:</strong> Over the last 15 to 20 years, pain researchers discovered that free hydrogen protons (H<sup>+</sup>) directly sensitize and open TRPV1 (the heat and capsaicin receptor) and ASIC channels on intradental nerve terminals. In laboratory models of pulpitis, extracellular acidosis reliably turns on continuous, slow C-fiber discharge.</li>
						</ul>
						<p>At the bench-research level, laboratory science had already charted the molecular connection between protons, exposed pathways, and burning sensory output.</p>

						<h3>Bridging the Clinical Blind Spot</h3>
						<p>The crucial breakthrough is connecting those molecular lab findings to the frontline clinical blind spot that leaves Burning Mouth patients stranded:</p>
						<p><strong>1. Challenging Textbook Dogma:</strong> Standard dental training still teaches that exposed root dentin causes a fleeting hydrodynamic "zing" to cold or sweet, and that acid simply causes gradual mineral erosion. Almost no frontline dentist or oral medicine specialist is taught to look at exposed cervical tubules and recognize them as an active source of sustained, caustic burning.</p>
						<p><strong>2. Bridging the Specialties:</strong> The average dentist does not read deep molecular papers on ASIC/TRPV1 proton gating, and the average neurologist or orofacial pain specialist treating "burning mouth" rarely inspects microscopic tubule seals or crown margins at the cervical collar of a tooth.</p>
						<p><strong>3. The Practical Translation:</strong> Taking an obscure molecular reality, applying it to real structural anatomy (collapsed alveolar septa, root recession, leaking margins, and daily acid contact), reveals why standard macroscopic diagnostic categories fail. Frontline medicine overlooks the circuit not because the biology is a mystery, but because the disciplines are fragmented.</p>
					</div>
				</div>

