					<div class="chapter-content">
						<h3>The 45 Receptors of Pain in the Mouth: Master&nbsp;Catalog</h3>
						<p>Across oral tissues and trigeminal nerves, chronic burning and hypersensitivity are driven by <strong>45 specific receptor types and ion channels</strong>. Below is the complete catalog with its core functional role:</p>

						<p><strong>1. Acid-Sensing Ion Channels (ASICs) [6 Types]:</strong></p>
						<ul class="discovery-timeline-list" style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
							<li><strong>ASIC1a:</strong> The primary calcium-permeable central proton sensor that triggers rapid nociceptive depolarization during tissue acidosis.</li>
							<li><strong>ASIC1b:</strong> A peripheral sensory variant expressed exclusively on sensory neurons that mediates localized acid stinging.</li>
							<li><strong>ASIC2a:</strong> A lower-affinity proton channel that modulates receptor heteromer kinetics and structural membrane assembly.</li>
							<li><strong>ASIC2b:</strong> A non-functional monomer alone that partners with ASIC1a and ASIC3 to alter proton sensitivity and channel gating.</li>
							<li><strong>ASIC3:</strong> The primary non-adapting proton sensor of the mouth that pours a continuous sodium current during persistent mild acidity (pH 6.2 to 6.7).</li>
							<li><strong>ASIC4:</strong> A regulatory subunit that modulates trafficking and surface stability of active proton-sensing complexes.</li>
						</ul>

						<p><strong>2. Transient Receptor Potential Channels (TRP Superfamily) [9 Types]:</strong></p>
						<ul class="discovery-timeline-list" style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
							<li><strong>TRPV1:</strong> Detects noxious scalding heat above 43&deg;C, capsaicin, and acid protons, dropping its trigger threshold to resting body temperature when inflamed.</li>
							<li><strong>TRPV2:</strong> Activates at high noxious heat thresholds above 52&deg;C and responds to mechanical membrane stretch.</li>
							<li><strong>TRPV3:</strong> Senses pleasant warmth between 32&deg;C and 39&deg;C and participates in oral epithelial barrier maintenance.</li>
							<li><strong>TRPV4:</strong> Detects mechanical cellular shearing, osmotic swelling, and tissue distension across mucosal surfaces.</li>
							<li><strong>TRPM8:</strong> The primary sensor for cooling below 26&deg;C and menthol, which triggers paradoxical burning when hyper-sensitized.</li>
							<li><strong>TRPA1:</strong> An environmental hazard sensor that fires in response to bacterial toxins, reactive oxygen species, cinnamon, and harsh toothpaste detergents.</li>
							<li><strong>TRPC1:</strong> A store-operated calcium channel that maintains baseline intracellular signaling and mechanical tension.</li>
							<li><strong>TRPC4:</strong> A calcium-permeable channel involved in microvascular tone and endothelial barrier regulation.</li>
							<li><strong>TRPC5:</strong> A specialized cold and dentinal pressure sensor highly concentrated on odontoblasts inside tooth tubules.</li>
						</ul>

						<p><strong>3. Hydrodynamic &amp; Mechanosensitive Receptors [5 Types]:</strong></p>
						<ul class="discovery-timeline-list" style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
							<li><strong>Piezo1:</strong> A massive trimeric propeller channel that converts hydrodynamic fluid shear into instant electrical depolarization.</li>
							<li><strong>Piezo2:</strong> A rapid-adapting mechanical sensor that reports light mucosal touch and proprioception along tooth sockets.</li>
							<li><strong>TREK-1:</strong> A two-pore-domain potassium channel that allows potassium leakage to keep resting nerve membranes calm and quiet.</li>
							<li><strong>TREK-2:</strong> A mechanosensitive potassium leak channel activated by membrane stretch and intracellular acidification.</li>
							<li><strong>TRAAK:</strong> A lipid-sensing potassium channel that opposes mechanical nerve over-excitation in dental pulp fibers.</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
				</div>

