					<div class="chapter-content">
						<p><strong>4. Purinergic ATP Pain Receptors [8 Types]:</strong></p>
						<ul class="discovery-timeline-list" style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
							<li><strong>P2X1:</strong> An ATP-gated cation channel mediating fast mechanical signaling and vascular contraction in oral tissues.</li>
							<li><strong>P2X2:</strong> A non-desensitizing ATP receptor that maintains steady sensory reporting in response to sustained cellular stress.</li>
							<li><strong>P2X3:</strong> The primary dental pulp ATP receptor that sparks instant pulpal throbbing when stressed odontoblasts release ATP.</li>
							<li><strong>P2X4:</strong> An ATP-activated channel on microglial and sensory cells that drives chronic neuropathic hypersensitivity.</li>
							<li><strong>P2X5:</strong> A structural ATP receptor that coordinates epithelial cell differentiation and barrier formation.</li>
							<li><strong>P2X7:</strong> A high-threshold ATP gate that triggers the NLRP3 inflammasome and releases massive cascades of inflammatory cytokines.</li>
							<li><strong>P2Y1:</strong> A G-protein coupled nucleotide receptor that mobilizes intracellular calcium stores in gingival fibroblasts.</li>
							<li><strong>P2Y2:</strong> An ATP/UTP receptor that stimulates mucosal fluid secretion and wound-healing responses after barrier breaches.</li>
						</ul>

						<p><strong>5. Voltage-Gated Sodium Amplifiers (Na<sub>v</sub>) [9 Types]:</strong></p>
						<ul class="discovery-timeline-list" style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
							<li><strong>Na<sub>v</sub>1.1:</strong> Mediates high-frequency action potential generation in myelinated mechanoreceptive fibers.</li>
							<li><strong>Na<sub>v</sub>1.2:</strong> Coordinates initial electrical impulse conduction along unmyelinated trigeminal axons.</li>
							<li><strong>Na<sub>v</sub>1.3:</strong> An embryonic sodium channel that re-emerges following peripheral nerve trauma to drive spontaneous firing.</li>
							<li><strong>Na<sub>v</sub>1.4:</strong> The primary voltage-gated channel responsible for muscle contraction in lingual skeletal fibers.</li>
							<li><strong>Na<sub>v</sub>1.5:</strong> The cardiac-type sodium channel also expressed in minor concentrations within peripheral sensory neurons.</li>
							<li><strong>Na<sub>v</sub>1.6:</strong> Clustered at the nodes of Ranvier to provide rapid saltatory electrical signal propagation.</li>
							<li><strong>Na<sub>v</sub>1.7:</strong> The master threshold trigger of sensory neurons that converts small receptor depolarizations into explosive nerve spikes.</li>
							<li><strong>Na<sub>v</sub>1.8:</strong> A tetrodotoxin-resistant sodium channel on C-fibers that sustains continuous action potential firing during cold and acidosis.</li>
							<li><strong>Na<sub>v</sub>1.9:</strong> Produces a persistent sodium current at resting voltages that amplifies sub-threshold inflammatory pain signals.</li>
						</ul>

						<p><strong>6. Inflammatory &amp; Barrier Defense Receptors [8 Types]:</strong></p>
						<ul class="discovery-timeline-list" style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
							<li><strong>TLR4:</strong> Recognizes bacterial lipopolysaccharides (LPS) from dental biofilm and mobilizes immune alarms.</li>
							<li><strong>CGRP Receptor (CALCRL/RAMP1):</strong> Binds calcitonin gene-related peptide to induce profound neurogenic vasodilation and tissue throbbing.</li>
							<li><strong>NK1 (Neurokinin-1):</strong> The primary receptor for Substance P that fuels neurogenic extravasation and local edema.</li>
							<li><strong>Bradykinin B1:</strong> An inducible pain receptor expressed during chronic ongoing inflammation to maintain heightened sensitivity.</li>
							<li><strong>Bradykinin B2:</strong> A constitutive receptor that mediates instant sharp pain and capillary permeability following acute tissue irritation.</li>
							<li><strong>Histamine H1:</strong> Triggers immediate itching, vasodilation, and sensory sensitization in mucosal membranes.</li>
							<li><strong>Prostaglandin EP2:</strong> Couples to adenylate cyclase to lower the thermal activation threshold of neighboring TRPV1 channels.</li>
							<li><strong>Prostaglandin EP4:</strong> Drives retrograde inflammatory signaling that amplifies trigeminal sensory transmission in the brainstem.</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
