					<div class="chapter-content">
						<h3>The Progression: Tracking What Makes It Worse</h3>
						<p>As you have seen in my story, I had no medical textbook to explain this at the beginning. I only knew that my mouth felt like it was scalded with boiling water every waking day. But when I systematically tracked what made the burn flare and what made it quiet down, one unmistakable biological pattern emerged: every single breakthrough came from eliminating an acid, and every single flare came from reintroducing&nbsp;one.</p>

						<p>Recognizing the acid connection did not happen overnight. It unfolded through a relentless process of progressive elimination. At first, I simply noticed undeniable correlations in my daily symptom log: whenever acidic beverages touched my mouth, the burning spiked within 30 to 90 minutes and lingered for&nbsp;hours.</p>

						<p>First it was beer, which carries a triple threat of low pH (4.0 to 4.5), effervescence, and alcohol. Next it was morning coffee, bathing my oral mucosa in chlorogenic and citric acids (pH 4.8 to 5.2). Then it was fruit, acidic dressings, and eventually fermentable carbohydrates that oral biofilm bacteria rapidly convert into lactic and acetic acids against tooth necks and gumline&nbsp;margins.</p>

						<p>Cutting beer was my first breakthrough. Once beer was gone, my peak pain flares dampened slightly, but persistent burning remained. That led me to examine my morning ritual: coffee. Coffee is packed with chlorogenic, caffeic, and citric acids, measuring between pH 4.8 and 5.2. Every morning, I was effectively bathing my sensitized nerve endings in warm acid. Eliminating coffee brought another noticeable drop in daily nerve&nbsp;irritation.</p>

						<p>Next came fruits. Sufferers are often told to eat more fruit for health, yet citrus, berries, apples, and tomatoes deliver intense doses of citric, malic, and ascorbic acids. Furthermore, fruit delivers concentrated fructose, which fuels subgingival bacterial fermentation. Eliminating fruit lowered my baseline burn even&nbsp;further.</p>

						<p>Finally, I stripped away acidic condiments, salad dressings, vinegars, and then vegetables containing fermentable carbohydrates that oral bacteria convert into acid. When every dietary acid and fermentable carb was eliminated on an all-meat carnivore protocol, the relentless fire in my mouth quieted down to a stable zero out of&nbsp;ten.</p>
					</div>
				</div>

