					<div class="chapter-content">
						<h3>The Community Data: Proof That I Am Not the Only&nbsp;One</h3>
						<p>Discovering that eliminating acid eliminated my pain was life-changing, but I needed to know whether my recovery was an isolated personal fluke or a shared biological reality across the wider Burning Mouth Syndrome&nbsp;community.</p>

						<p>To find out, I ran a targeted poll inside our Burning Mouth Syndrome Facebook support community, asking fellow sufferers: <em>"I am curious to know how many of us have noticed that acidic foods and drinks (coffee, wine, soft drinks, tomato sauce, citrus) make the burning&nbsp;worse."</em></p>

						<p>Seventy-seven patients participated, and the community data proved conclusively that <strong>I am not the only&nbsp;one:</strong></p>
						<p><strong>75% Answered YES (58 of 77 participants):</strong> Three out of every four patients confirmed that acidic foods and beverages directly worsen or ignite their oral&nbsp;burning.</p>
						<p><strong>16% Answered NO (12 participants):</strong> Did not observe an obvious link between dietary acid and their symptom&nbsp;spikes.</p>
						<p><strong>9% Answered OTHER (7 participants):</strong> Noted mixed observations, delayed 48-hour flares, or had not tracked food acidity&nbsp;yet.</p>

						<figure class="book-figure">
							<img src="/facebook-poll-acidity-trigger.png" alt="Facebook Community Poll Results showing 75% of 77 BMS participants confirming acidity makes burning worse" loading="lazy" />
							<figcaption class="book-figure-caption">
								<strong>Figure 3: Community Acidity Poll (n=77).</strong> Three out of four Burning Mouth Syndrome patients report that acidic foods and beverages directly intensify their oral&nbsp;burning.
							</figcaption>
						</figure>

						<h3>Why Eliminating Acid Reveals What Makes It&nbsp;Better</h3>
						<p>If something makes the pain worse, can doing less of what makes it worse reduce the pain below your baseline? In engineering and clinical troubleshooting, identifying the primary amplifier of a signal is half the solution. If pouring acid on sensitized nerve endings and exposed tooth conduits drives an intense flare, then completely removing that acid input removes the continuous chemical stimulus. Doing less of what harms the tissue gives peripheral receptors their first genuine opportunity to rest, desensitize, and return to a quiescent baseline&nbsp;state.</p>

