					<div class="chapter-content">
						<h3>The Three Anatomical Zones Where Acid Meets Nerve&nbsp;Endings</h3>
						<p>Although sufferers often perceive their mouth as engulfed in a single, diffuse firestorm, sensory neurobiology and dental materials science reveal that oral burning originates across three distinct physical entry&nbsp;points:</p>

						<p><strong>1. Exposed Soft Tissue and Oral Mucosa (Tongue, Palate, Cheeks, and Outer Gums):</strong> Your oral mucosa is a specialized living barrier lined with unmyelinated C-fiber nerve endings sitting mere micrometers beneath the surface. Under normal conditions, a continuous blanket of mucin-rich, bicarbonate-loaded saliva coats this tissue, buffering dietary acids and lubricating every tissue glide. When this mucosal shield becomes thinned, dehydrated, or stripped of its salivary film (xerostomia), free hydrogen protons (H<sup>+</sup>) make direct contact with superficial nerve endings across the palate, lateral tongue, and gingiva. Without resting salivary flow to dilute and buffer acids, even mild organic acids cling directly to unshielded tissue while normal mechanical tongue friction shears across sensitized&nbsp;receptors.</p>

						<p><strong>2. Internal Dentinal Tubules at Restorative Hardware Interfaces:</strong> A healthy natural tooth crown is sealed by crystalline enamel, which contains zero nerves. Beneath that enamel sits dentin, perforated by 20,000 to 45,000 microscopic, fluid-filled channels (dentinal tubules) per square millimeter leading straight into the living dental pulp. Even in meticulously placed dentistry, all restorative materials are subject to thermal cycling, chewing fatigue, and cement hydrolysis over a 10 to 15 year service life. Sub-micron marginal micro-gaps can develop along the interface of aging fillings and crowns. Capillary action draws oral fluids and bacterial acids down these margins into open internal tubules, stimulating pulpal nociceptive fibers from the inside out, even when the tooth appears clinically intact on a standard 2D&nbsp;X-ray.</p>

						<p><strong>3. Exposed Cervical Root Tubules and the Gingival Sulcus:</strong> Where the protective enamel crown terminates at the cervical margin, the tooth root is shielded only by a thin layer of cementum (20 to 50 microns thick). When gums recede by even 1 to 2 millimeters, or when mechanical friction and ambient acidity strip that fragile cementum away, tens of thousands of root tubules are left wide open to the oral environment. This exposure also extends inside the gingival sulcus and pocket gutters shielded from bulk saliva circulation. Here, anaerobic bacterial fermentation outpaces natural crevicular fluid clearance, bathing unarmored root dentin below its critical demineralization threshold (pH 6.2 to 6.7) and gating pulpal ASIC&nbsp;tripwires.</p>

