					<div class="chapter-content">
						<h3>Where Does the Acid Come&nbsp;From?</h3>
						<p>Where does the acid come from that triggers these dense receptor arrays and open dentinal tubules? In our engineering and biological framework, oral acid originates from two primary groups: external sources and internal&nbsp;sources:</p>

						<p><strong>1. External Acid Sources:</strong> External acids enter the oral cavity from the outside world through two distinct mechanisms: direct acids and fermentable sugars. Direct acids arrive in beverages, citrus fruits, vinegars, coffee, wine, and acidic mouthwashes, immediately delivering free hydrogen protons upon contact. Even more pervasive, however, are fermentable sugars and refined carbohydrates (bread, pasta, sweets, fruit, and starchy vegetables). While carbohydrates do not taste acidic, resident oral bacteria (<em>Streptococcus mutans</em> and <em>Lactobacillus</em>) consume trapped sugar residues and transform them into concentrated lactic and acetic acids directly against tooth margins and gumlines for 48 to 72 hours after a single&nbsp;meal.</p>

						<p><strong>2. Internal Acid Sources (From the Stomach):</strong> Internal acids rise upward from the digestive system. In Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD) and nocturnal Laryngopharyngeal Reflux (silent LPR), concentrated hydrochloric acid (pH 1.5 to 3.0) and proteolytic pepsin travel up the esophagus while sleeping flat. This nocturnal acid mist coats the posterior tongue, palate, and lingual molar margins, bathing vulnerable tissues in caustic stomach acid without necessarily causing classic chest&nbsp;heartburn.</p>

