			<section class="book-page" id="page-47b">
				<div class="page-body-content">
					<div class="page-running-head">
						<span class="chapter-header-label">Chapter 13: The Trigeminal Circuit: Referred Pain &amp; Cross-Talk</span>
						<span class="page-count-label">Page 47b</span>
					</div>

					<div class="chapter-content">
						<h3>The Real Physical Backfire: Antidromic Conduction and Neuropeptide&nbsp;Spraying</h3>
						<p>While conscious sensation travels orthodromically toward the brainstem, overloaded sensory C-fibers possess no internal electrical diodes. When high-frequency action potentials generated by acid protons hitting ASIC3 channels reach an axonal branch point (bifurcation), the electrical wave splits. Part of the wave travels up to the brainstem, but a powerful pulse travels <em>backward</em> (<span class="sci-molecule">antidromic conduction</span>) down neighboring branches into entirely uninjured oral&nbsp;tissues.</p>
						<p>When this antidromic pulse reaches terminal mucosal nerve endings, it triggers immediate calcium-dependent exocytosis of potent inflammatory neuropeptides: <strong>Substance P</strong> and <strong>Calcitonin Gene-Related Peptide (CGRP)</strong>. These neuropeptides degranulate mast cells, dilate local arterioles, and induce neurogenic plasma extravasation. This explains why a microscopic acid leak under an aging lower molar crown can physically trigger genuine chemical heat, throbbing, and raw redness across healthy anterior tongue and buccal mucosa where no mechanical wound&nbsp;exists.</p>
