					<div class="chapter-content">
						<h3>Real-World Analogies in Other Parts of&nbsp;the&nbsp;Body</h3>
						<p>This phenomenon, where an overloaded nerve circuit spills over into adjacent lines, is not unique to Burning Mouth. It occurs throughout human biology and modern&nbsp;engineering:</p>
						<p><strong>1. The Heart Attack (The Classic Arm and Jaw Radiator):</strong><br />
						<em>What happens:</em> The heart muscle is ischemic (starved of oxygen), but the patient rarely points with a single finger to the center of their chest. Instead, they feel a crushing ache radiating down the inner left arm, the neck, or the left&nbsp;jaw.<br />
						<em>Why it happens:</em> Visceral pain fibers from the heart enter the spinal cord at levels T1 to T5, which is the exact same switchboard where somatic sensory wires from the left arm and chest enter. The spinal cord pools these inputs onto shared relay neurons. Because the brain's map assumes that skin and muscles are injured far more often than internal organs, it resolves the heart's emergency as pain in the left&nbsp;arm.</p>
						<p><strong>2. The Kidney Stone (Groin and Thigh Radiation):</strong><br />
						<em>What happens:</em> A sharp mineral stone scratches the narrow ureter tube in the lower back (T11 to L2). Yet the patient feels intense pain shooting into their groin, testicle, or inner&nbsp;thigh.<br />
						<em>Why it happens:</em> As the ureter spasms, high-frequency signals flood the lower thoracic and upper lumbar spinal cord. Neurotransmitters spill across shared dorsal horn neurons, causing the brain to map the agony to the dermatomes of the groin and&nbsp;leg.</p>
						<p><strong>3. Sciatica and Lumbar Disc Herniation:</strong><br />
						<em>What happens:</em> A herniated disc at L5 pinches a single spinal nerve root in the lower back. The patient might feel little or no back pain; instead, their calf feels on fire, the outer foot tingles, and the sole of the foot&nbsp;throbs.<br />
						<em>Why it happens:</em> Even though the physical pinching and inflammation are located high up at the spine, the entire pathway mapped to that nerve root lights up, projecting sensory symptoms down to the terminal endpoints in the&nbsp;foot.</p>
					</div>
				</div>

