					<div class="chapter-content">
						<h3>Ephaptic Transmission, Central Wind-Up, and The 72-Hour&nbsp;Delay</h3>
						<p>Beyond synaptic convergence, severe chronic oral burning involves two critical biophysical phenomena established in recent sensory&nbsp;neurobiology:</p>
						<p><strong>1. Ephaptic Transmission (Direct Axon-to-Axon Cross-Talk):</strong> Unmyelinated trigeminal C-fibers travel bundled tightly inside common Schwann cell sheaths (Remak bundles). Under sustained chemical irritation, inflammatory edema strips protective myelin and increases extracellular potassium (K<sup>+</sup>). Action potentials in one active fiber create local extracellular voltage fields strong enough to directly depolarize silent adjacent fibers without any synapse. A firestorm in a single molar C-fiber literally sparks electrical fires across neighboring tongue and palate axons&nbsp;laterally.</p>
						<p><strong>2. The 72-Hour Bacterial Fermentation Clock:</strong> Why do so many BMS sufferers report that eating sugar or starch causes zero pain during the meal, but triggers an unbearable 10/10 firestorm two to three days later? Direct dietary acid acts in seconds, but fermentable carbohydrates feed anaerobic bacteria (*Streptococcus mutans* and *Lactobacillus*) trapped inside subgingival pockets and hardware micro-gaps. It takes 48 to 72 hours for bacterial biofilm to complete metabolic fermentation, accumulate concentrated lactic acid below pH 5.5, and breach open tubule conduits. Understanding this 72-hour delay dissolves the confusion of "random" flares and reveals the strict cause-and-effect chemistry of the&nbsp;disease.</p>
						<p><strong>3. The 2,000&times; Kinetic Demineralization Mismatch:</strong> Acid demineralization occurs in minutes, whereas salivary calcium remineralization takes weeks. Over decades, this creates an irreversible erosion deficit that permanently exposes deep pulpal&nbsp;transducers.</p>
					</div>
				</div>

