					<div class="chapter-content">
						<h3>The Forensic&nbsp;Takeaway</h3>
						<p>When clinical guidelines view chronic burning strictly through a central idiopathic lens, treatment focuses primarily on central neuromodulators (such as gabapentin, pregabalin, or clonazepam) to dampen dorsal horn wind-up and central&nbsp;sensitization.</p>
						<p>Central sensitization is a real neuroplastic adaptation that occurs when peripheral sensory pathways fire continuously over months or years. Neuromodulators provide meaningful symptom dampening by calming this central volume knob. However, dampening the alarm while leaving peripheral micro-chemical leaks actively fueling the circuit leaves the underlying fire burning at&nbsp;full&nbsp;strength.</p>
						<p>Nerves do not wind up into an unceasing central gain surge on their own. Central sensitization requires continuous&nbsp;peripheral&nbsp;fuel.</p>
						<p>When you remove fermentable carbohydrates and acidic liquids, you stop the proton flood at the ASIC tripwires. When a skilled dentist repairs a compromised margin or seals an exposed cervical root, you put physical armor back over&nbsp;the&nbsp;transducers.</p>
						<p>Once the peripheral telemetry goes quiet, the Subnucleus Caudalis stops overflowing, the signal crosstalk fades, and the widespread fire across your tongue and palate settles back down&nbsp;to&nbsp;zero.</p>
						<p>Having decoded the first principles of how open micro-pipes, leaking restorative seals, and bacterial lactic acid trip the trigeminal pain circuit, we examine how medicine formally defines this condition. In Part IV, we dissect the official BMS Standard Diagnosis Model: understanding the recognized criteria of inclusion, the mandatory battery of exclusion tests, and why a clean bill of health is the ultimate proof of neuropathic&nbsp;pain.</p>
					</div>
