					<div class="chapter-content">
						<h3>3. The ABAB Single-Case Experimental Design (SCED)</h3>
						<p>To conclusively prove whether an input is a genuine trigger or a coincidental correlation, document your tests using formal Single-Case Experimental Design (ABAB&nbsp;testing):</p>
						<ul>
							<li><strong>Baseline (A):</strong> Document your stable pain score and frequency of burning episodes under your normal daily routine for 7 to 14 days.</li>
							<li><strong>Intervention (B):</strong> Eliminate a suspected input (for example, coffee, wine, or all high-acid foods) or introduce an alkalizing protocol. Track the biological response over 14 to 21 days until a new baseline emerges.</li>
							<li><strong>Reversal (A):</strong> Re-introduce the specific input in a controlled manner. If the exact same burning pattern re-emerges within your documented 72-hour latency window, you have established strong biological causality.</li>
							<li><strong>Re-Intervention (B):</strong> Remove the input once again. When the fire settles back down, you possess rigorous, reproducible evidence that eliminates all subjective doubt.</li>
						</ul>

						<h3>4. Turning Your Ledger into Clinical Armor for Providers</h3>
						<p>When you sit in a dental or medical chair and state, <em>"My mouth burns all the time and nobody knows why,"</em> providers are clinically trained to suspect psychosomatic distress or central neuropathy. But when you present a clean, chronological binder showing:</p>
						<ol>
							<li>A 30-day VAS pain graph correlating flares with 72-hour post-carbohydrate fermentation windows,</li>
							<li>A precise map identifying tooth #19 and #30 gingival margins as the focal epicenters of burning, and</li>
							<li>Objective relief logs following topical baking soda rinses or temporary barrier varnishes,</li>
						</ol>
						<p>Your care team instantly shifts from dismissive reassurance to active technical partnership. Next, in Chapter 19, we apply the formal <strong>BMS Diagnostic Questionnaire</strong> to systematically score your symptoms and baseline vulnerabilities.</p>

