							<div class="book-callout-text" style="font-size: 0.88rem; line-height: 1.55; color: #4c1d95;">
								<strong>Medical &amp; Nutritional Disclaimer:</strong> Please consult your physician or a registered dietitian before starting any significant dietary elimination. Every human metabolism is unique; individual tolerances vary, and personal trial and error is an expected part of the&nbsp;investigation.
							</div>
						</div>

						<p>If you want to test whether dietary or bacterial acid is an active trigger for your burning mouth pain, you have two distinct paths. You can begin directly with a complete zero-acid reset, or you can start with a gentle low-acid trial. Both approaches serve as diagnostic instruments rather than permanent nutritional dogma.</p>

						<p>Every individual is biologically different. Even within clean whole foods, personal sensitivities exist: one person thrives on beef while another feels better with poultry or wild fish. Navigating an elimination trial requires patient observation, personal trial and error, and professional guidance when&nbsp;appropriate.</p>

						<h3>Option 1: Zero-Acid Elimination Trial</h3>
						<p>If you want to test if acid is a trigger for you, there is, as far as I know, one diet that is completely zero-acid: <strong>the carnivore diet</strong>. Because animal tissues contain virtually zero free plant acids and zero fermentable starches, eating exclusively animal-based whole foods cuts off the chemical fuel completely at the&nbsp;gumline.</p>

						<p><strong>What Goes on the Safe Plate (Zero-Acid Foods):</strong></p>
						<ul>
							<li><strong>Fresh Meats:</strong> Ground beef, ribeye, strip steaks, tenderloin, veal, lamb, pork, and nutrient-dense organ meats.</li>
							<li><strong>Poultry:</strong> Skinless chicken breast, roast turkey, duck, and pasture-raised fowl.</li>
							<li><strong>Wild Fish &amp; Seafood:</strong> Fresh salmon, cod, halibut, haddock, shrimp, and scallops (unseasoned, free from lemon juice or marinades).</li>
							<li><strong>Pasture-Raised Eggs:</strong> Boiled, poached, scrambled, or lightly fried in clean animal fat.</li>
							<li><strong>Clean Cooking Fats:</strong> Grass-fed butter, ghee, beef tallow, suet, and duck fat (strictly avoiding seed and vegetable oils).</li>
							<li><strong>Hydration:</strong> Clean, balanced, mineralized spring water (neutral pH 7.0 to 7.8; avoid acidic reverse-osmosis or distilled water at pH 5.5).</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
				</div>

