					<div class="chapter-content">
						<h3>Option 2: The Gentle Low-Acid Elimination Trial</h3>
						<p>For many individuals, jumping straight into a strict carnivore diet feels socially or psychologically daunting. Fortunately, you can start with a <strong>Gentle Low-Acid Elimination Trial</strong>. This option allows you to test whether reducing acidity quietens your pain while maintaining a wider, plant-inclusive menu.</p>

						<p>The goal is straightforward: eliminate all high-acid foods and aggressive chemical irritants while keeping foods that sit close to a neutral pH (6.5 to 7.4) and contain minimal fermentable&nbsp;starches.</p>

						<p><strong>What Goes on the Low-Acid Safe Plate:</strong></p>
						<ul>
							<li><strong>Clean Proteins:</strong> Fresh beef, skinless chicken breast, roast turkey, baked white fish (cod, halibut), and pasture-raised eggs.</li>
							<li><strong>Fresh Avocado:</strong> Gentle, rich in mucosal lubricating fats, and sitting at a neutral pH (6.2 to 6.6) without acidic sting.</li>
							<li><strong>Fresh Whole Mushrooms:</strong> White button or cremini mushrooms. They possess a neutral pH and structural beta-glucans that oral bacteria cannot ferment into lactic acid.</li>
							<li><strong>Firm Unfermented Tofu:</strong> Mild, unsweetened, non-fermented organic tofu providing neutral plant protein.</li>
							<li><strong>Neutral Cooked Vegetables:</strong> Steamed zucchini, peeled cucumbers, steamed cauliflower florets, and baby spinach.</li>
							<li><strong>Healthy Fats:</strong> Extra virgin olive oil, grass-fed butter, and ghee for cooking and seasoning.</li>
						</ul>

						<h3>Strictly Forbidden During the Low-Acid Trial</h3>
						<p><strong>Direct Acidic Foods:</strong> Citrus fruits (lemons, limes, oranges, grapefruit), vinegar, hot sauce, tomatoes, wine, coffee, and carbonated beverages.</p>
						<p><strong>Fermentable Starches &amp; Sugars:</strong> Breads, crackers, pasta, rice, sweets, and pastries. Even non-sour starches are fermented by oral bacteria into localized lactic acid within 48 to 72&nbsp;hours.</p>

						<p><strong>Expected Outcome:</strong> Most low-acid adherents experience a noticeable 40% to 60% reduction in burning intensity within 10 to 14 days, providing clear proof that oral pain is tied to dietary&nbsp;acidity.</p>
					</div>
				</div>

