			<!-- Chapter 24: The Recovery Protocol (Page 1 of 3) -->

			<section class="book-page" id="page-60">
				<div id="chapter-24" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
				<div id="ch24-settling" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
				<div id="ch24-emergency" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>

				<div class="page-body-content">
					<div class="chapter-opener-header">
						<div class="chapter-opener-num">Chapter 24</div>
						<h2 class="chapter-title chapter-opener-title">What If You&nbsp;Cheat?</h2>
						<div class="chapter-opener-divider"></div>
					</div>

					<div class="chapter-content is-first-page">
						<p>Cheating does not mean you have failed. Cheating can only show you one undeniable truth: <strong>acid makes things worse</strong>.</p>

						<p>When you eat a trigger food, drink a glass of wine, or give in to a carbohydrate craving, you are not destroying your body or starting back at zero. You are simply running an inadvertent scientific reversal test. Every single flare that follows a cheat meal is living, biological proof that your pain is not an unprovable mystery: it is the direct physical consequence of chemical fuel touching unshielded nerve&nbsp;terminals.</p>

						<h3>My Story: It Took a Full Year to Stop Cheating</h3>
						<p>I know the temptation of cheating intimately because it took me an entire year before I completely stopped. For twelve grueling months, I was trapped in an agonizing mental loop of bargaining, relapse, and regret.</p>

						<p>I would eat strictly plain meat for two or three weeks, feel my burning drop down from an unbearable 10/10 to a blessed, calm 1/10 or 0/10, and then the psychological bargaining would begin:</p>

						<p><em>"Maybe my nerves have healed now. Maybe I can handle just one craft beer with friends."</em></p>
						<p><em>"Why does everyone else get to eat sourdough bread, drink coffee, and enjoy pasta with zero pain, while a single roasted vegetable ruins my whole week? It isn't fair. I shouldn't have to live like this."</em></p>

						<p>I would give in. I would drink the beer or eat the meal. And on Day 1, nothing would happen. Because of the 72-hour bacterial fermentation latency delay, I would falsely believe I had gotten away with it. But by Day 3, the bacterial biofilm inside my periodontal sulci had finished fermenting those carbohydrates into concentrated lactic acid. I would wake up in the middle of the night with my tongue, palate, and gums immersed in a scalding 10/10 firestorm.</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="page-number-footer">
					<span>60</span>
				</div>
