			<!-- Chapter 24: What If You Cheat? (Page 2 of 2: Radical Acceptance & Emotional Peace) -->

			<section class="book-page" id="page-61">
				<div class="page-body-content">
					<div class="page-running-head">
						<span class="chapter-header-label">Chapter 24: What If You Cheat?</span>
						<span class="page-count-label">Page 61</span>
					</div>

					<div class="chapter-content">
						<h3>The Emotional Turmoil and the Exhaustion of Bargaining</h3>
						<p>The hardest battle in chronic illness is not physiological: it is deeply emotional. That constant internal tug-of-war created an exhaustion that was often far worse than the burning itself. Every cheat was followed by guilt, then the terrifying anticipation of the Day 3 flare, followed by days of anger, isolation, and depression in the dark.</p>

						<p>I had to grieve the loss of my old relationship with food. Eating is social, cultural, and deeply comforting. Stepping outside standard food rituals feels isolating. But every time I cheated, the fire returned with exact, mathematical predictability, demonstrating over and over that the circuit does not care about fairness, cravings, or social occasions: when you feed the bacteria, they make acid; when acid touches exposed tubules, your nerves scream.</p>

						<h3>The Practice of Radical Acceptance</h3>
						<p>Real, lasting healing began the day I surrendered to <strong>Radical Acceptance</strong>. Radical acceptance means letting go of how you wish your body worked, and accepting the objective biological reality of how it works right now:</p>
						<ul>
							<li>My mouth has receded root collars and aging margins that expose dentinal tubules to fluids.</li>
							<li>My oral bacteria will rapidly ferment starches into lactic acid if I feed them.</li>
							<li>My unmyelinated C-fibers will faithfully report that acid as an emergency alarm every single time.</li>
						</ul>

						<p>Once you stop fighting those biological facts, dietary discipline stops feeling like a punishment and transforms into profound self-respect. Passing on the bread basket or ordering plain steak at a restaurant is no longer deprivation: it is an empowering conscious decision to protect a calm baseline of peace. Living without pain is infinitely sweeter than any temporary treat.</p>

						<div class="book-callout" style="margin-top: 1.15rem;">
							<span class="book-callout-icon" aria-hidden="true">
								<svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
