					<div class="chapter-content">
						<h3>Carnivore as the Ultimate Zero-FODMAP Protocol</h3>
						<p>This is where the carnivore elimination diet enters the clinical picture: <strong>a pure carnivore diet is fundamentally the ultimate, absolute zero-FODMAP protocol</strong>. Meat, animal fats, and eggs contain <strong>zero fermentable plant sugars, zero fructans, zero galacto-oligosaccharides, zero polyols, and zero fermentable starches</strong>. By stripping away every single fermentable carbohydrate, you are not merely doing a "low-sugar" diet; you are completely depriving oral Streptococcus mutans and anaerobic subgingival biofilm of any substrate to synthesize lactic acid.</p>

						<p>Burning Mouth Syndrome sits at this exact historical crossroads today. Just as IBS patients ferment carbohydrates in the gut to irritate visceral small fibers, BMS patients ferment carbohydrates in periodontal sulci and exposed dentinal tubules to irritate trigeminal small fibers. Eliminating fermentable fuel is not dietary dogma; it is proven biophysical science starving the fire at its microbial&nbsp;source.</p>

						<h3>The Medical Blueprint: The Diabetic Parallel</h3>
						<p>If you step outside the dental operatory and walk down the hall into an endocrinology or neurology clinic, burning small-fiber pain is neither mysterious nor rare. It is seen every single day in millions of patients suffering from Diabetic Peripheral Neuropathy (DPN). Diabetic neuropathy is the premier medical blueprint for chronic small-fiber&nbsp;pain.</p>

						<p>When you place a diabetic patient complaining of burning in their feet side by side with a Burning Mouth patient complaining of fire in their palate, you are looking at the exact same neurological hardware screaming under the exact same biochemical rules:</p>
						<ul>
							<li><strong>Identical Sensory Fingerprint:</strong> Scalding heat, sandpaper friction, electric zings, and mechanical allodynia (where light touch, like a bedsheet on the toes or a tongue resting on teeth, feels like an open wound).</li>
							<li><strong>Identical Unmyelinated Cables:</strong> Both conditions involve unmyelinated C-fibers and thinly myelinated A&delta; fibers whose terminal membranes sit completely unprotected in the local extracellular fluid.</li>
							<li><strong>Identical Ion Channels:</strong> Both conditions are driven by hyper-excitable Na<sub>v</sub>1.7 sodium channels, TRPV1 capsaicin/heat receptors, and Acid-Sensing Ion Channels (ASIC1 and ASIC3) locking open in response to local acidity.</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
				</div>

