					<div class="chapter-content">
						<h3>The Two Bottlenecks: Systemic vs. Local Acid Gateways</h3>
						<p>If diabetic neuropathy and BMS share identical sensory wiring, why does one burn in the feet while the other burns in the mouth? The answer comes down to <strong>how the lactic acid fuel arrives</strong>:</p>

						<p><strong>1. The Systemic Bottleneck (Diabetes Type 1 &amp; Type 2):</strong> Chronic hyperglycemia and Advanced Glycation End-products (AGEs) occlude the microscopic capillaries (vasa nervorum) that feed peripheral nerves. Deprived of oxygen, nerve endings in the longest extremities (the feet) shift into anaerobic metabolism, manufacturing lactic acid from within. The foot burns because starved blood vessels produce local acid internally.</p>

						<p><strong>2. The Local Gateway (Burning Mouth Syndrome as "Type 6 Diabetes"):</strong> In BMS, systemic blood glucose and HbA1c can be completely normal. You do not need vascular failure when you have millions of exposed dentinal tubules and deep gum pockets connecting the oral cavity directly to pulpal C-fibers. When carbohydrates enter the mouth, salivary amylase breaks them into simple sugars within seconds. Subgingival bacteria (Streptococcus mutans and Lactobacilli) ferment those sugars into concentrated lactic acid directly over unshielded root margins. Instead of acid accumulating internally from starved capillaries, bacteria pump acid externally down exposed tubules.</p>

