					<div class="chapter-opener-header">
						<div class="chapter-opener-num">Chapter 31</div>
						<h2 class="chapter-title chapter-opener-title">The Top Questions &amp; Answers on Burning Mouth&nbsp;Syndrome</h2>
						<div class="chapter-opener-divider"></div>
					</div>

					<div class="chapter-content is-first-page">
						<p>Across thousands of patient case histories and community research logs, sufferers repeatedly encounter the same clinical paradoxes and unanswered diagnostic questions. Here are the clear, first-principles answers to the most critical questions in Burning Mouth&nbsp;Syndrome.</p>

						<h3>1. Why do far more women develop BMS than men, and why do men get it&nbsp;too?</h3>
						<p>Epidemiological registries document a dramatic female-to-male ratio of roughly five-to-one to seven-to-one, surging higher in postmenopausal cohorts. This occurs because the oral mucosa and salivary glands are dense with functional estrogen receptors (ER-&alpha; and ER-&beta;). When circulating estradiol plummets during perimenopause and menopause, oral epithelial skin thins by 20% to 30%, stripping the cellular cushion and bringing unmyelinated nociceptive C-fibers much closer to surface friction. Concurrently, resting salivary bicarbonate (HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>) output and protective mucins (MUC5B, MUC7) collapse, depriving the mouth of its primary chemical buffer and physical&nbsp;lubrication.</p>
						<p>Crucially, <strong>men develop BMS too</strong> because oral burning is not an exclusive hormonal disease; it is a multi-factor threshold failure. Men experience decades of dental cement washout under old crowns, micro-leakage around composite fillings, severe gumline recession exposing up to 45,000 dentinal tubules per square millimeter, nocturnal mouth breathing, and acid exposure (coffee, alcohol, GERD). When hardware leaks and acid overwhelm resting salivary reserves, the trigeminal alarm fires with the exact same 10/10&nbsp;severity.</p>
					</div>
				</div>

