			<!-- Chapter 31: The Top Questions & Answers on Burning Mouth Syndrome (Page 2 of 8: The 50-Year Curve) -->
			<section class="book-page" id="page-78b2">
				<div id="ch30-fifty" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
				<div id="page-34" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
				<div id="faq-fifty" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>

				<div class="page-body-content">
					<div class="page-running-head">
						<span class="chapter-header-label">Chapter 31: Top Questions &amp; Answers</span>
						<span class="page-count-label">Page 85b</span>
					</div>

					<div class="chapter-content">
						<h3>2. Why does Burning Mouth Syndrome so frequently start around 50 years&nbsp;old?</h3>
						<p>The fire does not materialize out of thin air; it is the culmination of a slow-motion mechanical and biological convergence fifty years in the making. In childhood and adolescence, chronic mouth breathing and juvenile decay lead to vaulted palates, dental crowding, and first-generation fillings, initiating subtle nightly drying. Through your twenties and thirties, clenching, crowns, and natural gum recession unshield 20,000 to 45,000 microscopic dentinal tubules per square millimeter along root collars. Meanwhile, restorative dental cements have a finite lifespan of 15 to 25 years before salivary acids dissolve their sub-micron&nbsp;margins.</p>
						<p>At age fifty, two separate vulnerability curves collide: cumulative hardware wear and tubule exposure reach their peak surface area at the exact moment natural aging and perimenopause reduce resting salivary volume and bicarbonate buffer capacity. When the mouth loses its chemical buffer right as structural defenses are breached, everyday acids flood into open tubules, locking pulpal Acid-Sensing Ion Channels (ASIC3 and TRPV1) into continuous neuropathic&nbsp;firing.</p>
