					<div class="chapter-content">
						<h3>3. Why does oral burning so frequently begin right after a tooth&nbsp;extraction?</h3>
						<p>Developing burning mouth symptoms following a tooth extraction is driven by four compounding biological&nbsp;mechanisms:</p>
						<p><strong>Surgical Deafferentation and the Phantom Alarm:</strong> When a tooth is extracted, the microscopic neurovascular fibrils entering the root apex are mechanically torn and sheared. The brainstem (<em>Subnucleus Caudalis</em>) loses its normal resting inhibitory braking input from the pulp, interpreting the total loss of peripheral signals as a catastrophic injury and turning up internal synaptic&nbsp;gain.</p>
						<p><strong>Traumatic Neuroma Formation:</strong> In the healing bone socket, severed axonal sprouts become trapped in dense scar tissue, creating a "frayed wire" that abnormally clusters voltage-gated sodium channels (Na<sub>v</sub>1.7, Na<sub>v</sub>1.8) and fires spontaneous ectopic electrical bursts into the&nbsp;jawbone.</p>
						<p><strong>Collateral Damage to Neighboring Teeth:</strong> Bone remodeling naturally causes gum recession off adjacent tooth collars, unshielding 20,000 to 45,000 open dentinal tubules per square millimeter right beside the socket. Everyday food acids flood into these newly exposed pipes, pouring fuel into an already traumatized&nbsp;circuit.</p>
						<p><strong>Referred Sensory Convergence Across Adjacent Teeth:</strong> In many cases, sensory signals cross talk across shared trigeminal branches. Burning symptoms may be experienced near one tooth while the true sub-micron cement microleakage or exposed tubules exist on an adjacent tooth. When adjacent margins remain unsealed, the fire persists until every microscopic peripheral opening is methodically identified and&nbsp;repaired.</p>
					</div>
