					<div class="chapter-content">
						<h3>4. How can an exposed margin on a single molar burn the roof of the mouth and tongue&nbsp;tip?</h3>
						<p>Clinicians are taught that dental pain is localized: an exposed root on a lower molar should produce a sharp zinger in that specific tooth, not a diffuse scald across the anterior palate or tongue tip. The bridge between a localized tooth leak and widespread whole-mouth fire is governed by the neuroanatomy of <strong>Spatial Summation</strong> and <strong>Convergence-Projection</strong>:</p>
						<p><strong>Convergence onto WDR Neurons:</strong> Pulpal sensory afferents from the Mandibular (V<sub>3</sub>) and Maxillary (V<sub>2</sub>) branches do not route into isolated private wires. Instead, thousands of tooth fibers converge onto shared Wide Dynamic Range (WDR) second-order neurons inside the <em>Subnucleus Caudalis</em>.</p>
						<p><strong>Glial Pump Overload:</strong> When an unsealed margin or receded collar leaves dentinal tubules exposed to everyday acids, living pulpal C-fibers fire continuous action potentials. In the synapse, this barrage releases massive volumes of glutamate and Substance P, overwhelming local glial astrocytic reuptake pumps (GLT-1 and EAAT2).</p>
						<p><strong>Volume Transmission (Neurotransmitter Spillover):</strong> Because astrocytic pumps cannot clear the flooded synapse, excess glutamate physically spills into adjacent neural columns, bathing dormant synapses that process telemetry from the anterior tongue (lingual nerve) and hard palate (nasopalatine nerve).</p>
						<p><strong>Cortical Mismapping:</strong> When neighboring WDR neurons depolarize from chemical spillover, they transmit emergency pain signals up the spinothalamic tract to the somatosensory cortex (S1). Because the cortex receives a flood across shared pathways, it maps the alarm across the entire anterior oral&nbsp;vault.</p>
					</div>
