						<h3>5. Why does burning vanish while eating, but return when you&nbsp;stop?</h3>
						<p>A frequent patient observation is: <em>"When I eat, my symptoms vanish, but return when I stop. How could burning be related to acid?"</em> Temporary relief during meals does not disprove an acid connection; it is explained by three distinct physiological&nbsp;mechanisms:</p>
						<p><strong>1. Gate Control Theory (Sensory Overdrive):</strong> Chewing activates high-threshold pressure receptors and mucosal touch fibers, firing thousands of high-velocity signals along thick A&beta; fibers into the <em>Subnucleus Caudalis</em>. This mechanical wave activates inhibitory interneurons, temporarily closing the neural pain gate against slow C-fiber burning&nbsp;telemetry.</p>
						<p><strong>2. The Alkaline Bicarbonate Flood:</strong> Under resting conditions, saliva flow trickles at 0.3 mL/min with modest buffering. Chewing triggers immediate parasympathetic stimulation, multiplying saliva output up to 3.0 mL/min and increasing bicarbonate (HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>) concentration by over 400% (pH 7.8 to 8.2). This alkaline rush neutralizes surface protons and closes proton-gated <strong>Acid-Sensing Ion Channels (ASIC1 and ASIC3)</strong>.</p>
						<p><strong>3. Bolus Shielding &amp; Delayed Rebound:</strong> Chewed food and viscous salivary mucins form an insulating physical coat over exposed root collars. Once swallowing ends, stimulated salivary flow plummets back to resting levels, the mechanical gate re-opens, and trapped food residues ferment into bacterial lactic acid, reigniting the burn on a delayed 24-to-72-hour&nbsp;curve.</p>
