						<h3>6. Why do some people have zero pain in the morning, progressing during the&nbsp;day?</h3>
						<p>Diurnal progression is a textbook hallmark of Burning Mouth Syndrome (traditionally categorized as Type 1 BMS). Patients wake up with calm tissues, only to watch symptoms build across the afternoon, peaking by evening. This pattern reflects three compounding daytime&nbsp;drivers:</p>
						<p><strong>Cumulative Proton Loading:</strong> During sleep, zero dietary acids enter the mouth. As the day unfolds, every beverage (coffee pH 5.0, tea), meal, and fermentable snack delivers a fresh wash of hydrogen protons (H<sup>+</sup>). Bacteria ferment trapped residues into lactic acid against unshielded tooth roots, continually activating <strong>Acid-Sensing Ion Channels (ASIC1 and ASIC3)</strong>.</p>
						<p><strong>Glial Saturation and Synaptic Wind-Up:</strong> Overnight, second-order neurons rest while glial pumps (GLT-1 and EAAT2) clear residual excitatory transmitters. As peripheral nerves fire continuous action potentials across the day, the synaptic cleft is flooded with glutamate and Substance P. By late afternoon, glial reuptake pumps reach capacity, generating <strong>central wind-up</strong> that turns routine signals into an evening scald.</p>
						<p><strong>Speech Micro-Friction:</strong> The tongue swallows roughly 800 times daily and moves constantly during speech. Over hours, micro-friction cycles drag lingual mucosa across tooth edges and restoration margins. On a sensitized, hyper-innervated oral mucosa, mechanical contact is perceived as burning heat (allodynia). Sleep allows tissues to rest and synapses to reset for morning.</p>
