					<div class="chapter-content is-first-page">
						<p>When institutions fail to bridge the divide, the responsibility shifts to the people with skin in the game: the patients living with&nbsp;the&nbsp;fire.</p>
						<p>This is the purpose of the citizen-science initiative behind Decoding&nbsp;the&nbsp;Burn.</p>
						<p>Historically, medical research trickled down from academic ivory towers. A pharmaceutical company funded a trial, clinicians tested a synthetic molecule, and after fifteen to twenty years, a new treatment guideline slowly emerged&nbsp;into&nbsp;textbooks.</p>
						<p>In rare, complex, or cross-disciplinary conditions like Burning Mouth Syndrome, that traditional top-down model has completely stalled. Decades of research have yielded little more than clinical trials testing whether gabapentin, clonazepam, or alpha-lipoic acid can marginally reduce pain scores in a cohort of&nbsp;exhausted&nbsp;sufferers.</p>
						<p>We do not have fifteen years to wait for an academic consensus to notice that exposed dentin leaks acid into the&nbsp;trigeminal&nbsp;system.</p>
						<p>Through modern digital platforms, open-access patient registries, and shared symptom tracking, we can build a bottom-up epidemiological&nbsp;evidence&nbsp;base:</p>
						<p>Systematic Flare Logging: When thousands of patients track their symptom scores against daily inputs, the 48-to-72-hour bacterial fermentation latency clock ceases to be an individual anomaly and becomes a validated, statistically&nbsp;significant&nbsp;pattern.</p>

					</div>
